To create a Chrome extension that saves the contents of the current webpage, you'll need to follow these steps:

1. **Set up your project structure**: Create a new directory for your extension and inside it, create two files: `manifest.json` and `content.js`.

2. **Create the manifest file** (`manifest.json`): This file contains metadata about your extension and specifies its permissions and background scripts.

```json
{
  "manifest_version": 3,
  "name": "Save Page Content",
  "version": "1.0",
  "description": "Saves the content of the current webpage.",
  "permissions": [
    "activeTab",
    "storage"
  ],
  "background": {
    "service_worker": "background.js"
  },
  "action": {
    "default_popup": "popup.html",
    "default_icon": {
      "16": "icons/icon16.png",
      "48": "icons/icon48.png",
      "128": "icons/icon128.png"
    }
  }
}
```

3. **Create the content script** (`content.js`): This script will be injected into web pages and will handle saving the page content.

```javascript
// content.js

function savePageContent() {
  const body = document.body;
  const html = body.innerHTML;

  // Save the HTML content to local storage or send it back to the popup
  chrome.runtime.sendMessage({ action: 'saveContent', data: html });
}

document.addEventListener('DOMContentLoaded', () => {
  // Add an event listener to trigger the save function when needed (e.g., button click)
  document.getElementById('saveButton').addEventListener('click', savePageContent);
});
```

4. **Create the background script** (`background.js`): This script handles messages from the content script and can manage any additional logic if necessary.

```javascript
// background.js

chrome.runtime.onMessage.addListener((request, sender, sendResponse) => {
  if (request.action === 'saveContent') {
    // Handle the saved content here
    console.log('Saved content:', request.data);

    // Optionally, store the content in local storage or perform other actions
    localStorage.setItem('savedContent', request.data);
  }
});
```

5. **Create the popup UI** (`popup.html`): This is where users interact with the extension. It includes a simple interface to trigger the content saving process.

```html
<!-- popup.html -->

<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
  <meta charset="UTF-8">
  <title>Save Page Content</title>
  <style>
    /* Basic styling */
    body { font-family: Arial, sans-serif; padding: 10px; }
    #saveButton { margin-top: 10px; padding: 5px 10px; cursor: pointer; }
  </style>
</head>
<body>
  <h1>Save Current Page Content</h1>
  <button id="saveButton">Save Content</button>

  <script src="popup.js"></script>
</body>
</html>
```

6. **Add some basic JavaScript to the popup** (`popup.js`): This script injects the content script into the active tab and sets up the button click handler.

```javascript
// popup.js

document.addEventListener('DOMContentLoaded', () => {
  const saveButton = document.getElementById('saveButton');

  saveButton.addEventListener('click', async () => {
    const [tab] = await chrome.tabs.query({ active: true, currentWindow: true });

    chrome.scripting.executeScript({
      target: { tabId: tab.id },
      files: ['content.js']
    }).then(() => {
      console.log('Content script injected');
    }).catch(error => {
      console.error('Error injecting content script:', error);
    });
  });
});
```

7. **Load the extension in Chrome**: Go to `chrome://extensions/`, enable Developer Mode, and click "Load unpacked". Select the folder containing your extension files.

This setup provides a basic framework for creating a Chrome extension that saves the content of the current webpage. You can expand upon this by adding more features such as saving to cloud services, providing options for different formats, etc.

